\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Model and Predictor.}
We evaluate on LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, which uses SwiGLU FFN blocks~\cite{shazeer2020glu} with $d_{\text{model}}=4096$ and $d_{\text{ff}}=14336$ across 32 layers.
The sparsity predictor is a two-layer MLP with 2.37M parameters per layer (75.9M total), implemented as Linear($4096 \to 128$) + GELU + Linear($128 \to 14336$), trained to produce per-neuron importance scores that minimize output-distribution divergence under sparsity.

\paragraph{Training.}
All predictors are trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-103 with sequence length 2048 and batch size 4 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB), using AdamW~\cite{loshchilov2019adamw} with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay $1 \times 10^{-2}$, and cosine decay.
The KL predictor uses Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} with temperature $\tau=1.0$ annealed to $0.1$ over training.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity on WikiText-2~\cite{merity2017pointer} (seq\_len=2048) and downstream accuracy on ARC-Challenge (25-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), MMLU (5-shot), GSM8K (8-shot), HellaSwag (10-shot), and PIQA (0-shot).

\paragraph{Inference Protocol.}
At inference, the predictor produces per-neuron scores that are passed to TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal}, which distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly.
We term this \textit{predictor-TEAL}: the predictor replaces TEAL's magnitude-based scoring while retaining its allocation strategy.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against: (1) \textbf{Vanilla TEAL}~\cite{liu2025teal}, which uses activation magnitudes directly\footnote{TEAL is deterministic; single-run evaluation suffices. KL/BCE predictors require multi-seed evaluation due to random initialization.}; (2) \textbf{BCE predictor} (DejaVu-style~\cite{liu2023dejavu}), trained with per-neuron binary cross-entropy on magnitude-based oracle masks for 1000 and 2700 steps; and (3) \textbf{Uniform allocation}, which distributes the sparsity budget equally across layers.


\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison. Uniform allocation preserves downstream performance best; global allocation is analyzed to understand allocation-quality tradeoffs (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).
At 30\% sparsity, KL distillation under uniform allocation achieves PPL 8.81$\pm$0.02 (3-seed), reducing TEAL's perplexity by 19\% (10.82 $\to$ 8.81; 14.5\% at matched 28.5\% sparsity, Appendix~\ref{app:ood}). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, teal30_uniform
Uniform allocation yields stable downstream (MMLU 40.7$\pm$1.3\%, 3-seed; Appendix~\ref{app:stability}), while global top-$k$ exhibits high seed variance (35.7$\pm$9.5\%) from late-layer starvation (L31 retention 28.4\%; \S\ref{sec:scaling}). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform, kl30_3seed

\paragraph{Task-Type Split.}
KL outperforms TEAL on commonsense reasoning (ARC-c, WG, HellaSwag, PIQA: 4/4 wins at 30\% uniform) but underperforms on knowledge-intensive tasks (MMLU, GSM8K: 0/2).
Forward KL's mode-covering property ($\mathbb{E}_p[\log p/q]$) preserves diverse activation patterns that benefit broad commonsense inference; however, this may dilute task-specific knowledge pathways that magnitude-based methods preserve more faithfully (hypothesis; mechanistic investigation left to future work). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, teal30_uniform, hellaswag_piqa_kl30_uniform

At 50\% sparsity, the PPL advantage widens: 11.92 vs.\ 23.90 (50\% reduction over TEAL). % exp_id: bench_kl50_uniform, teal50_uniform
The predictor generalizes to C4 validation at both 30\% (PPL 16.03$\pm$0.01, 2-seed, global allocation) and 50\% (52.5\% over TEAL; Appendix~\ref{app:ood}).
However, all methods exhibit downstream collapse on MMLU (28.1$\pm$0.2\% vs.\ 28.0$\pm$0.6\%, both $\approx$ random, 3-seed) and GSM8K ($<$2.2\%); this is a capacity-limited regime where no allocation strategy can recover. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$; downstream accuracy$\uparrow$). \textbf{Bold}: best per sparsity. $^\ast$Extended training budget ($2.71\times$ KL step budget), no compensation; training details in \S\ref{sec:isocompute}. $^\dagger$BCE 30\%: TEAL global (2700 steps). $\pm$: 3-seed mean$\pm$std ($s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$); unmarked: seed 42. TEAL uses uniform allocation at all sparsity levels. MSE: per-layer FFN output reconstruction loss (ShadowLLM-style~\cite{akhauri2024shadowllm}; same architecture/data/optimizer, $\lambda_{\max}{=}1000$; Appendix~\ref{app:mse_config}), 3-seed PPL ($\sigma${=}1.68 vs.\ KL $\sigma${=}0.02); downstream: seed 42. $^\ddagger$Actual sparsity: KL 28.5\% vs TEAL 29.3\% at 30\% target; matched-sparsity comparison in Appendix~\ref{app:ood}. Both within 0.5pp at 50\%.}
\label{tab:main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{5}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL uniform & 10.82 & 39.33 & 65.04 & \textbf{45.86} & \textbf{8.79} \\ % exp_id: teal30_uniform
& BCE global$^\dagger$ & 11.25 & 41.2 & 66.0 & 36.3 & 6.0 \\ % exp_id: bce30_teal_global
& MSE uniform & 18.52$\pm$1.68 & 31.23 & 60.38 & 31.79 & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: mse30_uniform_s42
& KL global (Ours)$^\ddagger$ & 8.96$\pm$0.02 & \textbf{43.8$\pm$3.6} & \textbf{70.2$\pm$0.9} & 35.7$\pm$9.5 & 8.0$\pm$0.7 \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& KL uniform$^\ddagger$ & \textbf{8.81$\pm$0.02} & 43.7$\pm$2.5 & 68.9$\pm$0.4 & 40.7$\pm$1.3 & 7.5$\pm$0.1 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{5}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL uniform & 23.90 & 27.47 & 54.38 & \textbf{29.26} & 1.74 \\ % exp_id: teal50_uniform
& BCE (2700 steps)$^\ast$ & 28.13$\pm$0.20 & 27.6$\pm$0.6 & 51.8$\pm$0.6 & 26.1$\pm$0.5 & 2.1$\pm$0.2 \\ % exp_id: bce50_3seed
& MSE uniform & 229.26 & 25.00 & 50.59 & 23.79 & 1.59 \\ % exp_id: mse50_uniform_s42
& KL global (Ours) & \textbf{11.84$\pm$0.15} & 32.85 & \textbf{63.46} & 28.1 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, kl50_global_s123, kl50_global_s456
& KL uniform & 11.92$\pm$0.07 & \textbf{33.8$\pm$1.2} & 62.9$\pm$0.1 & 28.0$\pm$0.6 & \textbf{2.2$\pm$0.1} \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_uniform, bench_kl50_uniform_s123, bench_kl50_uniform_s456
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}


\subsection{Extended Training Analysis}
\label{sec:isocompute}

To test whether BCE simply requires more compute, we train it for 2700 steps ($2.71\times$ KL budget, matched wall-clock: $\sim$46 min vs.\ $\sim$45 min on RTX PRO 6000).
BCE achieves PPL 28.13$\pm$0.20 (3-seed), still $2.39\times$ worse than KL (11.79). % exp_id: bce50_3seed
The difference is systematic: BCE achieves +7pp oracle F1 (0.613 vs.\ 0.540) with 139\% worse PPL, and KL's near-random precision (0.51) indicates a mask optimized for distributional fidelity rather than magnitude agreement. % exp_id: f1_oracle_analysis, bce_isocompute_s42


\subsection{Mask Pattern Analysis}
\label{sec:mask_analysis}

To understand \emph{what} the KL predictor learns, we compare KL-derived masks against magnitude-based oracle masks (the BCE training targets) at 50\% sparsity on WikiText-2.
Per-layer Jaccard similarity averages 32\% at 50\% sparsity (64 samples, 131K tokens), computed per-token (each token's KL and magnitude masks are constructed independently, then Jaccard distances are averaged across tokens): the KL predictor discovers neuron importance patterns that diverge 68\% from activation magnitude, with divergence scaling from 46\% at 30\% to 68\% at 50\% (Figure~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}).
At layer 16, 41\% of neurons fall in disagreement quadrants where KL and magnitude rankings diverge (Appendix~\ref{app:mask_viz}, Figures~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}--\ref{fig:scatter}).
Despite this divergence, KL achieves PPL 11.79 vs.\ BCE's 28.13$\pm$0.20: output-distribution fidelity, not magnitude agreement, produces lower-perplexity masks under global allocation. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bce50_3seed, c1_mask_overlap

\paragraph{Per-Layer Divergence Structure.}
Per-layer Jaccard distance follows a U-shaped pattern (Figure~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}): middle layers show lowest divergence ($\sim$0.61 at 50\%), while late layers peak at 0.70--0.75, consistent with stronger output-sensitivity gradient signal near the KL-optimized distribution. % exp_id: c1_mask_overlap

\paragraph{Target-Swap Ablation: Target Quality vs.\ Gradient Coupling.}
To disentangle whether KL's advantage stems from its coupled gradient pathway or from the mask patterns it discovers, we train a standard BCE predictor on KL-derived oracle masks instead of magnitude-based masks (C1 target-swap ablation).
Table~\ref{tab:c1} reports results under TEAL uniform allocation at matched sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Target-swap ablation (C1): BCE on KL-derived masks vs.\ KL predictor (same seed), TEAL uniform. All seeds show max $\Delta$$\leq$1.70pp at 30\%; max $\Delta$$\leq$0.79pp at 50\% (s42). $^\dagger$s42 oracle is from an earlier training run; s123/s456 use matched retrained oracles.}
\label{tab:c1}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lllccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Seed & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{30\%}
& s42$^\dagger$ & C1 & 9.45 & 40.27 & 67.40 & \textbf{42.30} & \textbf{7.58} \\ % exp_id: c1_ablation_30pct_s42_eval_v2
& & KL & \textbf{8.80} & \textbf{40.78} & \textbf{68.59} & 40.6 & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
& s123 & C1 & 8.84 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.77} & \textbf{39.92} & 6.82 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s123
& & KL & \textbf{8.83} & \textbf{45.31} & 69.38 & 39.4 & \textbf{7.51} \\ % exp_id: kl30_s123_eval
& s456 & C1 & 8.82 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.93} & 41.04 & 6.60 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s456
& & KL & \textbf{8.79} & \textbf{45.05} & 68.75 & \textbf{42.0} & \textbf{7.58} \\ % exp_id: kl30_s456_uniform_eval
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{50\%}
& s42 & C1 & 11.96 & 32.17 & \textbf{63.14} & 27.79 & \textbf{2.05} \\ % exp_id: c1_bce_kl_targets_teal50u
& & KL & 11.96 & \textbf{32.59} & 62.35 & \textbf{27.92} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: c1_kl_teal50u
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At 50\%, C1 matches KL within 0.79pp, while magnitude-trained BCE yields $2.4\times$ worse PPL (Table~\ref{tab:main}).
At 30\%, retrained seeds show $\leq$1.18pp agreement; the s42 oracle from an earlier checkpoint has a 0.65pp gap but confirms the core finding.
Mask quality is the proximate driver of inference-stage quality; a per-layer KL control (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}) further shows that cross-layer coupling contributes marginally to PPL but substantially to downstream task fidelity. % exp_id: bce50_3seed, c1_bce_kl_targets_30u, c1_s123
PPL transfers within 0.79pp; downstream metrics exhibit larger seed-dependent variation (up to 4.5pp MMLU).

\paragraph{Soft-vs-Hard Confound.}
C1 also changes mask representation (hard vs.\ soft), but BCE+Gumbel yields PPL 32.22 vs.\ standard BCE 27.97 (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}): softness alone does not explain KL's advantage. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_no_penalty_s42, eval_bce_v3_s42

C1's oracle masks reflect a specific KL training trajectory; trajectory-dependent confounds are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:oracle_provenance}.

\subsection{Ablation Studies}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation} isolates the contribution of each design choice at 30\% and 50\% sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, unified benchmark protocol; uniform allocation at 30\%, global allocation at 50\%). 30\%: 3-seed mean$\pm$std ($s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$) where available; 50\%: seed 42. $^\ddagger$STE achieved only 20.8\% model-level sparsity at 30\% target due to tied sigmoid scores in the threshold-based mask; excluded from 30\% comparison (see \S\ref{sec:ablation_ste}).}
\label{tab:ablation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Configuration & 30\% PPL$\downarrow$ & 50\% PPL$\downarrow$ & $\Delta_{50}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{KL-based variants}} \\
Forward KL + Gumbel (Ours) & \textbf{8.81$\pm$0.02} & \textbf{11.79} & --- \\ % exp_id: kl30_ablation_s42, bench_kl50_global
JSD (symmetric) + Gumbel & 8.90$\pm$0.02 & 12.54 & +0.75 \\ % exp_id: jsd30_ablation_s42, jsd_ablation_s42
Reverse KL + Gumbel & 9.26$\pm$0.07 & 12.80 & +1.01 \\ % exp_id: rkl30_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
Forward KL + STE & ---$^\ddagger$ & 15.77 & +3.98 \\ % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Per-layer training}} \\
Per-layer MSE & 18.52$\pm$1.68 & 229.26 & +217.47 \\ % exp_id: mse30_uniform_s42, mse50_uniform_s42
Per-layer KL + Gumbel & 9.05$\pm$0.02 & 34.85 & +23.06 \\ % exp_id: perlayer_kl_30_3seed, perlayer_kl_50_s42
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{BCE-based variants}} \\
BCE (no compensation) & 15.71 & 27.97 & +16.18 \\ % exp_id: bce30_uniform_s42, eval_bce_v3_s42
BCE + Gumbel (no penalty) & --- & 32.22 & +20.43 \\ % exp_id: bce_gumbel_no_penalty_s42
BCE + Gumbel + Penalty & --- & 38.02 & +26.23 \\ % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation
BCE + STE & --- & 60.54 & +48.75 \\ % exp_id: bce_ste_s42
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Relaxation, Divergence, and SPON.}
At 50\%, Gumbel-Sigmoid outperforms STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} by 33.8\% (11.79 vs.\ 15.77); forward KL outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with mode-covering~\cite{gu2024minillm}. % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42, jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
At 30\%, forward KL (8.81) and JSD (8.90) are near-identical, both outperforming reverse KL (9.26) and dramatically better than MSE (18.52$\pm$1.68; $\sigma$=1.68 vs.\ KL's $\sigma$=0.02, reflecting per-layer signal instability). % exp_id: kl30_ablation_s42, jsd30_ablation_s42, rkl30_ablation_s42, mse30_uniform_s42, mse30_3seed
BCE without compensation (15.71) confirms decomposed losses underperform at both regimes. % exp_id: bce30_uniform_s42
The near-equivalence of KL and JSD suggests the specific divergence measure matters less than whether the loss optimizes through the output distribution; within output-distribution losses, cross-layer scope provides downstream benefits not predicted by PPL alone (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}).
Reverse KL at 30\% (3-seed) reinforces this: commonsense (ARC-c 43.7$\pm$1.7\%, WG 69.0$\pm$1.1\%) exceeds TEAL while MMLU (39.2$\pm$2.5\%) falls below KL (40.7$\pm$1.3\%) and TEAL (45.9\%), showing the same commonsense-vs-knowledge asymmetry across all output-coupled losses---forward KL's mode-covering retains slightly more knowledge capacity. % exp_id: rkl30_downstream_3seed
\paragraph{Per-Layer KL: Decomposing Signal Type and Coupling Scope.}
\label{sec:perlayer}
To isolate cross-layer coupling, we train per-layer KL predictors that mask one random layer per step and compute KL on the full model output, preserving the output-distribution signal without cross-layer gradients.
Per-layer KL achieves 9.05$\pm$0.02, within 2.7\% of end-to-end KL (8.81$\pm$0.02), while per-layer MSE degrades to 18.52$\pm$1.68 ($2.05\times$ worse). Signal type\footnote{We use ``signal type'' as shorthand for the training objective's optimization target (output distribution vs.\ intermediate activations); this dimension also entails differences in gradient flow topology and optimization landscape that are not independently controlled.} accounts for 97.5\% of the PPL gap. % exp_id: perlayer_kl_30_3seed
However, downstream tasks reveal a dissociation: per-layer KL underperforms end-to-end KL by 3--5 pp on commonsense (ARC-c, WinoGrande, HellaSwag), while BoolQ shows the largest gap: per-layer KL achieves 64.5$\pm$11.5\% vs.\ 73.7$\pm$3.2\% for E2E KL (both 3-seed, uniform), a 9.2\,pp deficit with $3.6\times$ higher seed variance, suggesting cross-layer coupling is critical for stable binary reasoning. At 50\%, coupling scope's importance increases sharply: per-layer KL degrades to 34.85 vs.\ 11.92 for E2E KL (+192\%), with coupling's share rising from 2.5\% to 10.6\% ($4\times$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) and downstream falling to TEAL level---coupling transitions from optional refinement to essential coordination as sparsity increases. % exp_id: perlayer_kl_downstream, perlayer_kl_50_s42
The per-layer KL comparison isolates coupling scope but introduces a train--inference distribution shift: each training step masks only one random layer, while inference applies all 32 masks simultaneously. The observed downstream gap may reflect (a)~suboptimal masks from independent per-layer optimization, (b)~inference-time interaction effects among 32 simultaneously applied masks, or (c)~both. We cannot fully disentangle these factors with the current design.

Adding SPON~\cite{xu2025spon} bias correction contributes 2.4\% relative (Appendix~\ref{app:spon}). % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42

